  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\nIssued at 1018 PM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nAs of 9:30 PM Friday, a surface low pressure system continues to\npush across the Upper Mississippi Valley. An attendant cold front\nis situated over southeast Missouri, providing enough lift to\nencourage shower and thunderstorm development. The environment\nover this area is conducive to upscale storm growth with a nice\noverlap of thermodynamic and kinematic variables. Most notably,\nbulk shear is on the rise as a low level jet develops over the\nregion. Storms continue to exhibit severe characteristics with\nstorm tops located at about 53,000 ft. This activity will become\nmore linear in nature over the next hour or so and trek into the\nMissouri Bootheel and northeast Arkansas. Severe weather\ningredients in this region are less conducive due to the presence\nof convective inhibition. At 7:45 PM, an ACARS sounding out of\nthe Memphis International Airport sampled CIN at -109 J/kg. This\nfeature is being modeled well across CAMs, suggesting rapid\ndeintensification of storms as they move into the Mid-South. If\nstorms are able to overcome this cap, damaging winds would be the\nprimary threat. A secondary threat of a spin-up tornado cannot be\nruled out due to a veering wind profile near the surface.\nHowever, the probability of a tornado occurring is very low. The\nsevere weather threat may transition to heavy rainfall concerns\novernight due to precipitable water values near the 99th\npercentile. The REFS is beginning to pick up on an area of 2 to\n2.5 inches of rain falling near Greene and Clay counties in\nnortheast Arkansas overnight. This area will be at the greatest\nconcern for flooding through sunrise Saturday.\n\nBehind the cold front, pleasant conditions will encompass the Mid-\nSouth. High temperatures on Saturday will vary from north to\nsouth, in the low 80s to low 90s respectively. Northerly winds\nwill persist into Sunday, allowing seasonably cool air to reach\ninto central Mississippi. Sunday will resemble mid September as\nhighs remain in the upper 70s to mid 80s. Unfortunately, cooler\nconditions will not last long as upper level ridging builds into\nthe region by Tuesday. At this time, some guidance is developing\na cutoff low over the southeast. This feature will keep rain\nchances near the Tennessee River through Thursday, but the bulk\nof precipitation will remain over middle and east Tennessee.\nOtherwise, expect increasing temperatures with highs reaching the\nmid 90s by the end of the week. At this time, additional heat\nheadlines may be needed. Until then, enjoy the cooler weather.\n\nANS",
